@prefix orexis:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix progression: <http://example.org/orexis/progression#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix review: <http://example.org/orexis/review#> .
@prefix market: <http://example.org/orexis/market#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/market#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/market> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis market" ;
    rdfs:comment """The auction: a venue, a host that announces offers, bidders that answer, claims that come back, and the matching that turns the bids into an allocation with prices. What a bid is WORTH is domain-specific and lives in the domain module; how a round is conducted is here. THREE capabilities compose independently — an agent may bid, host, or do both, and a host also has whichever matching its world put in force. Matching is a family with interchangeable members where bidding and hosting are single abilities; it shares this package because a directory is a package and not a capability, and the seam it needs is `PROVIDES` and a term, not a directory boundary.""" .

#################  Capabilities  #################

:Bidding a orexis:Capability ; rdfs:label "Bidding" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent answers offers in a market it participates in, with a bid computed from its own private valuation. Nobody else can compute that bid; that is the point of one process per agent." .

:Hosting a orexis:Capability ; rdfs:label "Hosting" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent runs rounds in a market: announces the offer, collects bids until the deadline, matches, has the trade validated, and issues claims. The scarce side hosts." .

#################  The venue  #################

:Market a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Market" ;
    rdfs:comment "The standing STRUCTURE around a resource: what is allocated, who may supply it, who may consume it, and the channels they meet on (see transport), so participants discover the venue rather than being configured with it. A market is not an event — the AUCTION is the event, condensing out of scarcity and dissolving again, and a market with nothing contested is still a market with no auction in it. Both were called 'market' until recently and the two readings are opposites; see knowledge/domain/market.md." .

:marketFor a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Market ;
    rdfs:label "market for" ;
    rdfs:comment "The resource being allocated. The cluster boundary: who competes with whom." .

:aboutProperty a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:label "about property" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which observable property a bidder's DESIRE is expressed in — stated by the domain on the desire term itself, never on a market. THE BEARER IS A CONTRACT: the term that carries this is the domain's lot-per-property conversion, stated per subject as the physics and per agent as its belief — which is what lets a domain-neutral generator bind the term from this link and use it as a predicate, joining a dose to the reading it moves without naming any domain (see onboarding/compose.py). A second domain brings its own conversion term (watts per degree, litres per fraction), hangs this off it, and every generator and reflex follows. A market is a lot: 1L of water is 1L of water whether or not anyone's soil is dry, and a market for a thing no instrument measures (a time slot, a right of way) must remain expressible. What is genuinely property-shaped is the stake: a target of 0.55 is 0.55 OF something, and until this term existed that number was dimensionless. A bidder holding readings of several properties of one subject needs to know which one its valuation is about; it follows this link from the term it already names." .

#################  Which way the lot moves what it is priced in  #################
#
#  The other half of the claim `market:aboutProperty` makes, and the half that was written
#  NOWHERE: "buy water to raise moisture" was spread across the graph except for RAISE. The
#  reflex knew it only because `deficit = aim - value` hardcodes the sign — harmless with one
#  lever, and unwritable the moment anything needs a menu that says what each act DOES. A model
#  told only "there is a market for water" cannot know whether bidding helps a dry pot or a
#  soaked one. See knowledge/decisions/the-model-is-consulted-at-the-edge-of-knowledge.md.
#
#  Stated by the DOMAIN, on the same term that carries the denomination, because they are two
#  halves of one sentence: bids here are priced in this property, and winning moves it this
#  way. Deliberately just direction — cost-shape, magnitude and side-effects are speculation
#  until a second lever exists, and magnitude is already the denomination term's own value.

:Direction a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Direction" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which way applying a market's lot moves the property its bids are priced in. Two individuals and no third: a lot whose effect depends on circumstances is a lot whose domain should model the circumstances, not a shrug in the vocabulary." .
:Raises a :Direction ; rdfs:label "raises" .
:Lowers a :Direction ; rdfs:label "lowers" .

:offeredBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "offered by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The party whose standing offer this source is — the neutral word the venue derivation reads, with each domain bridging its own (water:suppliedBy is a subproperty, on the capacityL -> lotCapacity precedent). Together with the owner stating market:matchesBy, this is the whole premise of a shop: a source offered by someone who says how they match is a market, derived; everything else about the venue is minted from the source's id. See knowledge/decisions/a-market-arises-where-want-meets-supply.md." .

#################  The lot states its good (#198)  #################
#
#  What a venue's lot is a quantity OF, and the missing half of every denomination join in the
#  codebase. `market:aboutProperty` says which property a VALUATION is priced in; nothing said
#  which valuations belong to which VENUE, so the menu, the reflex and the participation rule
#  all joined "a market I can reach" to "a valuation about this property" as independent facts —
#  safe while one domain meant one denomination, and a cross-multiplication the moment a second
#  existed. The barrel plan's own arc 4 is that second: the city sells litres against
#  StoredLitres while the barrel sells them against SoilMoisture, and BOTH sources are water.
#
#  Which is why the tie routes through the GOOD and not from venue straight to valuation: what a
#  litre DOES depends on where it lands (a pot's moisture, a barrel's stock), so one good
#  honestly has several valuations — one per kind of recipient — and what disambiguates a venue
#  is what flows out of its source, not what any buyer will do with it. The source states its
#  good ONCE, at class level (a WaterSource vends water — the datasheet pattern, entailed onto
#  every barrel and every mains); each valuation term states which good it converts; and every
#  join closes venue -> marketFor -> source -> supplies -> good <- ofGood <- valuation, so two
#  opposite levers on one property each carry their own direction and never each other's.

:Good a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Good" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a lot is a quantity of — water, heat, airflow, a right of way. NOT a property: a good is what changes hands, a property is what a buyer hopes it moves, and conflating them is exactly the cross-join this class exists to end. Individuals are the domain's to declare (water:Water); the market only demands that a source names one." .

:supplies a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range :Good ;
    rdfs:label "supplies" ;
    rdfs:comment "The good a source holds — 'the lot states its good' (#198), stated where the lot is owned, because the lot IS the source's standing offer. Stated at CLASS level by the domain (every WaterSource vends water, by what the thing is) and reaching each source entailed, so no world restates it and no venue can disagree with its own source. The venue's tie is the composition marketFor/supplies — deliberately not minted onto the market node as a third triple, because a copy of a derivable fact is what one-word-for-one-relation exists to refuse." .

:ofGood a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range :Good ;
    rdfs:label "of good" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which good this valuation term converts into its property — the valuation's half of the venue tie. A pot's litres-per-fraction is a claim about WATER, not about liquids in general; a drying lever's minutes-per-fraction is a claim about airflow that happens to price the same property the other way. Stated on the same term that carries aboutProperty and direction, because the three are one sentence: a lot of THIS GOOD, priced in THIS PROPERTY, moves it THIS WAY." .

:direction a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range :Direction ;
    rdfs:label "direction" ;
    rdfs:comment "Applying this valuation's lot moves the priced property this way. What the reflex steers by instead of a hardcoded sign: gap below the aim and a lever that Raises — or above it and one that Lowers — is the move. A heater against a cold snap is the same rule with no code change, which is the whole point of stating it." .

:hosts a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range :Market ;
    rdfs:label "hosts" .
:bidsIn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range :Market ;
    rdfs:label "bids in" ;
    rdfs:comment "Composed in the world: participation is public, so a host knows who may bid. What the agent will PAY stays private." .

:offerTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Market ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "offer topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the host announces a round." .
:bidTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Market ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "bid topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where bidders answer. A prefix: each bidder publishes under its own localId." .
:claimTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Market ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "claim topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the host returns what was won. A prefix, one segment per winner." .
:redeemTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Market ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "redeem topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a HOLDER presents its claim. A prefix, one segment per holder — the mirror of claimTopic, and the channel that makes winning and actuating separate acts (#132): the host used to redeem every claim itself the moment it issued them, which spent the dose before the winner's sensor could possibly be watching it land. The holder now presents when its watch is live, and a spot market grew the futures market's wire a venue at a time." .

:redeemWindowS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Market ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "redeem window (s)" ;
    rdfs:comment """How long a winner has to present, from the moment its claim is issued. The venue's rule and therefore the venue's word: a market that offers redemption has to say how long it holds the good, or the difference between a holder still watching and a holder that walked away is unstateable — and a host with no deadline must either keep every claim for ever or spend the dose on its own guess.

It is what gives an obligation its urgency, which is the reason it exists now rather than as a tidy-up. A duty's heat is the room it has left: at issue there is the whole window and nothing has gone wrong, near expiry there is none and lateness is real. Without a stated deadline the only candidates were a proxy for lateness or a constant, and the record for obligations names both as the whole risk — always-hottest is the honoured mode by another name, never-hot is an agent that quietly defects.

Must comfortably exceed the longest a holder may wait before presenting, which is one full cycle of whatever cadence that holder's sensing currently commands (`_present_blind`'s bound). That is dynamic, so no shape can check it; a window shorter than a slow board's cycle expires claims the holder was never able to present in time, and the water stays in the barrel while the buyer is billed. When in doubt state it long: the cost of a generous window is a debt remembered slightly too long, and the cost of a mean one is a society that charges for water it never delivered.""" .

:lotCapacity a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "lot capacity" ;
    rdfs:comment "The physical ceiling of what a market's resource can supply — the constitution's total-allocation limit, read by clearing. Deliberately the MARKET's word (#148): the kernel loads a market's capacity and must name no domain, so a domain states its own term as a subproperty (water:capacityL does) and the closure entails this one — the same move mc:carries makes under sosa:hosts." .

:allocationCeilingL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "allocation ceiling" ;
    rdfs:comment "The most this participant may be allocated in ONE trade, whatever it bid — the constitution's per-participant limit, read by clearing beside the total. Deliberately the MARKET's word for the same reason :lotCapacity is (#148): the kernel must name no domain, and what counts as too much is a fact only a domain knows. Unlike :lotCapacity this cannot be a subproperty bridge, because the number is COMPUTED rather than stated and the subproperty closure is materialised before any rule runs — so a domain DERIVES this term directly, as water/rules.ru does from the span of a subject's survival range. STRUCTURAL, and that is the whole of why it is safe: it is the span the subject could ever legitimately traverse, not a headroom measured from where the subject is now. A host cannot see a bidder's state and must not need to — see decisions/agent-centric-epistemics.md. A participant whose subject states no survival range simply has no ceiling, and clearing checks nothing for it: absent is not zero." .

#################  The host's own terms (private belief)  #################

:offerQuantityL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "offer quantity litres" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the host puts up in a round." .
:reservePricePerL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "reserve price per litre" ;
    rdfs:comment "A genuine seller's floor: below this it would rather keep the resource. Private — buyers see the offer, never the reasoning." .
:roundCooldownS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "round cooldown seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "The host's floor on how often it will open a round, so a flapping participant cannot spam the market." .
:bidWindowS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "bid window seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long the host waits for bids before matching. A bid that misses the deadline is simply not in the round." .

#################  The bidder's own terms (private belief)  #################

:hasEndowment a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "has endowment" ;
    rdfs:comment "OPENING wallet balance, authored at genesis — what an agent brought to the venue, not what it has left. The running balance is :balance, the agent's own belief; the authoritative one would be the mint's, which is not built (knowledge/domain/wallet.md)." .

:balance a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "balance" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this agent has left, as its own belief in its own graph — written when a claim is won, read when a bid is priced. Absent until the first claim, which reads as the endowment: a wallet nobody has spent from is the wallet it was given. A BELIEF and not a pick: nothing chooses it, and review may not move it (the-wallet-is-a-belief-the-agent-keeps)." .

#################  Matching: the third capability, and the only family  #################
#  A family, because given a lot and a set of bids there is genuinely more than one right
#  answer, and which one is in force changes what a rational bidder should offer. That is the
#  test for a capability rather than a function: the HOW could differ. Bidding and hosting have
#  one way each and are therefore not families; all three are capabilities of this package.

:BidMatchingCapability a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Matching capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "The family. Anything that turns a lot and a set of bids into a proposed allocation with prices is one of these, so a host may ask for 'whoever matches' without knowing which member answered. The host PROPOSES; clearing still disposes — a matching capability decides an allocation, never whether it is permitted. Note the width of the term: a member supplies BOTH halves of what mechanism design calls a mechanism — an allocation rule (who gets how much) and a payment rule (what each winner pays) — but NOT how bidding proceeds. Ascending, descending and sealed-bid are the other half of what the literature calls an auction format, and that half is fixed here rather than pluggable — see knowledge/domain/bid-matching.md." .

:PayAsBid a :BidMatchingCapability ; rdfs:label "Pay as bid" ;
    rdfs:comment "Greedy and discriminatory: bids at or above the reserve are filled highest-price-first, each up to its own maximum and capped by what is left, and each winner pays ITS OWN bid. The v1 member. It rewards bidding your true value only as far as you must — a winner that bid well above the clearing point pays all of it, so the honest strategy is to shade, which is the standard objection to pay-as-bid and the reason the alternative is declared beside it." .

:UniformPrice a :BidMatchingCapability ; rdfs:label "Uniform price" ;
    rdfs:comment "The same allocation rule as pay-as-bid, a different payment rule. Allocated exactly as pay-as-bid allocates — highest first, capped by what is left — and then every winner pays ONE price: the lowest accepted bid, which is where the demand curve crossed the lot. Bidding your true value costs nothing, because your own bid sets your bill only if you turn out to be the marginal buyer. Its own weakness is the mirror of that: a bidder large enough to be marginal can shade its QUANTITY rather than its price, taking less in order to leave the margin at a cheaper bid and pay less on everything it does take — demand reduction, the standard objection to uniform price, as shading is to the other. The reserve is the floor rather than a fallback: a round whose demand never reaches the lot is a walk that never crossed supply, so everyone is filled and everyone pays the reserve, with no contested-or-not test anywhere. That is what makes issue #50 a choice of member rather than a defect to patch." .

#################  What a host states  #################

:matchesBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range :BidMatchingCapability ;
    rdfs:label "matches by" ;
    rdfs:comment "How this host matches bids in the auctions it convenes. The host defines the terms of its own auction, so this is the host's fact and not the venue's — and it is PUBLIC, in the world, because a bidder cannot bid well against terms it does not know. Real auctions announce theirs; a sealed-bid first-price auction says so. Named `matchesBy` rather than `clearsBy` deliberately: clearing is a different thing here — the validator that co-signs a trade — and reusing the word would have made a host's allocation read as the notary's business. The capability is DERIVED from this, never declared." .

#################  A round, as a FACT  #################
#
#  An open round lived in a dict on the host and in `pending` on the bidder, and on the wire —
#  nowhere any store could answer for. So the buying action's precondition could only say "I
#  could buy here", never "I can buy now", and nothing could plan an Offer because nothing
#  could see one. A round is a belief now, on BOTH sides (a-round-is-a-fact-and-offering-is-an-
#  action): the host writes one when it announces, the bidder when it hears, each into its own
#  graph, and each retracts it when the round is over for it. Nothing on the row is not already
#  on the wire — and two things must NEVER be: the host's `bidWindowS` and `roundCooldownS`, the
#  private beliefs that keep a bidder from timing its arrival. `closesAt` is what the offer
#  said; the window is how the host arrived at it.

:Round a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Round" ;
    rdfs:comment "One open round on a venue, as the agent holding this row was told it or announced it: the lot, the reserve and when bidding closes. Written on announce (host) or on offer (bidder) into that agent's own graph, retracted at close, and a row past its closesAt is swept on the next ask — so the walk `?venue market:hasRound ?r . ?r market:closesAt ?t FILTER(?t > NOW())` is the whole of 'a round is open'." .

:hasRound a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Market ; rdfs:range :Round ;
    rdfs:label "has round" ;
    rdfs:comment "The venue a round is open on. From the venue to the round, so the buying action's walk — venue, source, good, host, valve — takes one more hop and stops when there is nothing to hop to." .

:roundId a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Round ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "round id" ;
    rdfs:comment "The auction id on the wire, so a claim or a close can find the row it ends." .

:lotL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Round ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "lot, litres" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this round offers — the host's standing offer capped by its stock, as announced." .

:reservePerL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Round ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "reserve per litre" ;
    rdfs:comment "The reserve announced for this round." .

:closesAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Round ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "closes at" ;
    rdfs:comment "When bidding closes — the offer's closes_in_s, as an instant. The one clock a bidder is told; the host's window and cooldown stay its own." .

#################  A call — the want a LOW sources on a host  #################
#
#  A participant's LOW used to be a TRIGGER: hosting heard it, checked a cooldown and a dict,
#  and announced. Now that a round is a fact and offering is an action, the LOW is what makes
#  a round WANTED — a want the host did not source, as a claim makes a dose wanted
#  (an-obligation-is-a-desire-someone-else-sourced) — and the search plans it: Offer, or
#  acquire upstream then Offer when the vessel is dry. See knowledge/domain/call.md.

:Call a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Call" ;
    rdfs:comment "A round is wanted on this venue, because a participant said it is in trouble. One per venue, in the host's own graph, written on the LOW and retracted when a round opens there. Unmet while no round stands and no clock runs it down: a call is answered or it is not, and the search says what stands in the way." .

:calledOn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Call ; rdfs:range :Market ;
    rdfs:label "called on" .

:calledBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Call ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "called by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The participant whose verdict raised it — the last one, where several did. Prose for the log and the ask channel; a call is never sized from who called." .

:calledAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Call ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "called at" .

:coolingUntil a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Market ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "cooling until" ;
    rdfs:comment """THE VENUE IS COOLING, and this row's PRESENCE is that fact — written at close, from `roundCooldownS`, into the host's own graph, and retracted when the cooldown runs out. The Offering action's precondition asks whether the row is there and compares nothing (#598).

It was `mayConveneAt` until then, and the name is why it had to change rather than the filter: a row saying "may convene at 12:05" is TRUE the whole time, so its presence said nothing and every reader had to do the arithmetic — `?may <= NOW()`, one of the six clock reads deliberation used to make. Presence had to mean the state, so the term had to name the state.

The instant it carries is not for comparing. It is the HORIZON, exactly as a round's `closesAt` is: what a sweep reads to retract a row whose timer never landed, because a process restarts and a scheduler does not survive it. The private `roundCooldownS` is still never on a row anyone else could read.""" .

#################  A claim I hold  #################
#
#  What winning a round leaves me with, as a FACT in my own graph (#523): the bidder's
#  Python held it as a dict, so nothing in the ledger could wait on it. Now a claim arriving
#  is a triple, Tendering's `orexis:doneWhen` binds on it, and presenting marks it so the
#  same claim is never presented twice. What a claim IS on the host's side — an obligation,
#  a debt owed — is the ower's record; this is the buyer's copy of the fact that it won.
:Claim a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Claim" ;
    rdfs:comment "A lot a round cleared to this agent, held until presented: its id, its litres, its debit, the venue it came from, and when it was presented." .
:holdsClaim a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range :Claim ; rdfs:label "holds claim" .
:claimId a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Claim ; rdfs:range xsd:string ; rdfs:label "claim id" .
:claimL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Claim ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "claim litres" .
:claimDebit a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Claim ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "claim debit" .
:onVenue a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Claim ; rdfs:range :Market ; rdfs:label "on venue" .
:claimedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Claim ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ; rdfs:label "claimed at" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the round cleared to me — what a tender adopted before it is done by, and a stale claim from an earlier round is not." .
:presentedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Claim ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ; rdfs:label "presented at" ;
    rdfs:comment "When I presented it — or when the host redeemed it for me, on a venue with no redeem channel, written with the claim so presenting has nothing left to do." .

#################  How close a bought lot must land  #################
#
#  The effect an action states is ONE declaration (#510, #518): the reading the search planned
#  on is the reading the world is held to, and the actor sizes nothing of its own any more.
#  What is left to say is how close a number must land to count as the same effect, and that
#  is not physics — it is a belief about how good the conversion is, held like every belief
#  here: a PICK inside constitutional bounds, review moving it on evidence (residuals lean
#  one way: the conversion is wrong; they scatter: the tolerance is). A FRACTION of the
#  predicted movement, so it is scale-free across properties; below the floor sensor noise
#  reads as a lie, at 1.0 the band reaches back to the baseline and no movement at all would
#  pass, which is why the ceiling is there. No domain is stated, deliberately: an agent holds
#  its pick and the CAPABILITY holds the default an agent that states none gets, as
#  `progression:Intention` holds `suspectAfter`. The sensing grain as a floor is a seam (#518).
:tolerance a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "tolerance" ;
    rdfs:comment "How far, as a fraction of the movement a step predicted, an observed value may land from the predicted one and still count as the effect the search planned on. An agent's pick within :minTolerance and :maxTolerance; the capability carries the default." .
:minTolerance a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Capability ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "min tolerance" .
:maxTolerance a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Capability ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "max tolerance" .
:tolerance a review:RevisableBelief ; review:revisableToward :minTolerance , :maxTolerance .
:Bidding :tolerance 0.5 ; :minTolerance 0.05 ; :maxTolerance 1.0 .
